The master files
19-the-master-files
The top box says whether the master files are set up, what the journal holds, whether the stale or the resync flag is set, how many accounts were compared and how many differences there were, and why the last save of a master record in this session was refused. The list is every registered account with its state, in sync or the number of differences, and any account that has master copies but is not registered. The box under it lists the differences of the account under the cursor (missing, differs, extra, nonwritable, norights, toolong, unreadable) and says in plain words what they mean. The page reads again when it opens, after a sync and on F5, not on a timer: a check reads every master file and applies the journal.
S syncs every account: it sets the master files up if they are not, applies the journal, makes the master copy of every registered account match the account and removes the copies of accounts that are not registered. It is the only thing that clears the stale and resync flags. Y syncs the account under the cursor and does not clear them. Each shows its command and what it does and runs it only when you answer Run; Cancel is what Enter chooses. P shows the journal lines not yet applied, and back.
The page edits no master record and no account record: the sync only makes the master copies match the accounts. The verb asks for the administrator itself and writes its own audit line.
